@node Installation
@chapter Installation

libdtfl uses the GNU build system.  From a release tarball:

@example
./configure
make
make install
@end example

From a git checkout, generate the build system first with the bundled
bootstrap script, which runs the autotools:

@example
./bootstrap
./configure
make
make install
@end example

@command{make} builds the shared library @file{libdtfl.so}, the
command-line tools @command{dtfl-diag} and @command{dtfl-ddir}, this
manual (@file{libdtfl.info}), and the manual pages.  @command{make
install} installs the library, the public headers under
@file{dtfl/}, the @file{dtfl.pc} pkg-config file, the tools, the info
manual and the manual pages.

@section Configure options

@table @option
@item --with-hegel[=DIR]
Build and run the property-based tests, which use the Hegel testing
library.  Without it, the property tests report SKIP.  Give the install
prefix as @var{DIR}, or rely on @command{pkg-config} finding it.

@item --enable-debug
Build with assertions and the debug-only contract checks (the
worker-ownership checks of @ref{Threads}).

@item --enable-asan
@itemx --enable-ubsan
@itemx --enable-tsan
Build with the AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer or
ThreadSanitizer.  ThreadSanitizer verifies the thread-safety contract
over the multi-worker suites.

@item --enable-diag
@itemx --enable-ddir
@itemx --enable-server
Build the diagnostics console, the DDIR language and tooling, and the
diagnostics server component.  Each is on by default where its
prerequisites are present.

@item --enable-usdt
@itemx --disable-usdt
Build the USDT static probes (@pxref{Diagnostics}) for DTrace, SystemTap,
@command{bpftrace} and @command{perf}.  On by default when
@file{sys/sdt.h} is found (on Debian, the @code{systemtap-sdt-dev}
package); without it each probe compiles to nothing.
@option{--enable-usdt} fails when the header is missing.

@item --disable-frame-pointers
The library is built with @option{-fno-omit-frame-pointer} (and
@option{-mno-omit-leaf-frame-pointer} where the compiler accepts it), so
profilers can walk its stack in a release build; this option lets the
compiler omit them.  A frame-pointer flag in @env{CFLAGS} takes
precedence either way.
@end table

Link a program with @option{-ldtfl}, or let pkg-config supply the flags:

@example
cc myprog.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs dtfl)
@end example

@section Running the test suite

@command{make check} runs the unit, property-based and distribution
tests.  Each test runs under a time limit, @env{DTFL_TEST_TIMEOUT}
seconds (default 600; 0 turns it off): a test that hangs is killed and
reported as FAIL, with a line in its log saying it timed out, instead of
stalling the run.  The project's full gate, @command{dev/check-all}, builds and
tests six configurations, each in its own build directory:

@table @asis
@item default
assertions on (@option{--enable-debug}).
@item asan
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
(@option{--enable-asan --enable-ubsan}).
@item hegel
the property tests enabled (@option{--with-hegel}).
@item hegel-asan
the property tests under ASan and UBSan.
@item tsan
ThreadSanitizer (@option{--enable-tsan}).
@item distcheck
@command{make distcheck}: builds a distribution tarball and checks that
it unpacks, configures, builds, tests and ships the manual and the
manual pages.
@end table

A phase of the project is not finished until all six pass.

@subheading Simulator coverage

@command{make check-sim} runs the multi-worker property tests under the
simulation allocator (@code{DTFL_COMM_SIM}), which runs every worker on
one thread under a seeded scheduler.  Each test case draws its own
simulator seed, worker count and progress mode, and a fault set: worker
stalls (a worker skips a drawn number of scheduler turns) and delivery
delays (a message waits a drawn number of turns).  A delay holds back
everything behind the message on its (sender, receiver, channel) queue,
so per-channel FIFO order, which timely relies on, is kept; only
messages on different queues are reordered.  The properties cover the
communication layer, join, count, notification order and an iterative
bfs.

It needs a build configured @option{--with-hegel}; without it every
property reports SKIP and @command{make check-sim} fails with status 77,
so a qualification script cannot mistake a run that tested nothing for
a pass.  Two variables size the run:

@table @env
@item DTFL_SIM_SEEDS
test cases per property (default 2000);
@item DTFL_SIM_JOBS
parallel shards (default 1).  Shard @var{k} runs hegel with seed
@var{k}+1, so a run is reproducible from these two values.
@end table

For example, a release qualification run of 100,000 seeds per property
on 16 cores:

@example
DTFL_SIM_SEEDS=100000 DTFL_SIM_JOBS=16 make check-sim
@end example

Each property prints PASS, FAIL or SKIP.  A failure prints the minimal
case hegel shrank it to, including the simulator seed and faults, and a
@env{DTFL_PBT_REPLAY} command that reruns that one case.

@subheading Fuzzing

@command{make fuzz} is the libFuzzer tier.  It needs a build configured
with clang and @option{--enable-fuzz}, which compiles the library with
AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and fuzzer coverage; in
any other build @command{make fuzz} prints SKIP and exits 77.  There is
one target per decoder of bytes from outside the process: the TCP
message framing, the data and progress message codecs, capture and
replay events, the time and summary codecs, the timely, differential
and communication log records, recorded log streams as
@command{dtfl-diag} reads them, the diagnostics server's WebSocket
handshake, DDIR programs and values, and the record descriptors and
batch codec.  Where a decoder has an encoder, the target also checks
that decoding and re-encoding gives the input back.

Each target first replays every input committed under
@file{tests/corpus/fuzz/@var{target}/}, the seeds and the reproducer of
every crash fixed so far, and then fuzzes from them.  Two variables size
the run:

@table @env
@item DTFL_FUZZ_SECONDS
seconds of fuzzing per target (default 60; 0 replays the corpus only);
@item DTFL_FUZZ_JOBS
parallel fuzzing processes per target (default 1).
@end table

New corpus entries go to @file{fuzz-corpus/} in the build directory and
crashing inputs to @file{fuzz-out/}; neither touches the source tree.
@command{make fuzz-seeds} regenerates the seed corpus from the library's
encoders after a wire format changes.

@example
./configure CC=clang --enable-fuzz && make
DTFL_FUZZ_SECONDS=1800 DTFL_FUZZ_JOBS=16 make fuzz
@end example

@section Platform notes

The test suite, with each sanitizer, passes on these platforms:

@table @asis
@item Linux x86_64 and arm64 (Debian 13, gcc 14 and clang 19)
Everything, including ThreadSanitizer, the USDT probes (with
@code{systemtap-sdt-dev}) and the property tests (hegel publishes
@file{libhegel} for both).  The ThreadSanitizer tests run under
@command{setarch -R}, which configure applies.

@item FreeBSD 14 amd64 (base clang 21, and gcc 14 from ports)
Use @command{gmake}.  Both compilers build and pass @command{make
check} and @command{make distcheck}; clang passes under ASan, UBSan and
TSan, gcc under ASan and UBSan.  Where they differ from Linux:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The tests of a sanitizer build run with address randomization off,
through @command{proccontrol -m aslr -s disable}, which configure
applies.  gcc's @file{libasan} refuses to start under FreeBSD's ASLR
(``This sanitizer is not compatible with enabled ASLR''); to run a
gcc ASan program by hand, start it the same way.
@item
gcc has no ThreadSanitizer runtime on FreeBSD (the port ships no
@file{libtsan}); configure stops on @option{--enable-tsan} with gcc.  Use
clang.
@item
libthr allocates mutexes and condition variables with a private
allocator that ThreadSanitizer does not intercept, which TSan
misreports as a race inside @code{__thr_calloc}.
@file{tests/tsan.supp} suppresses it, and @command{make check} passes
it in @env{TSAN_OPTIONS}.  A program of yours run under TSan on FreeBSD
needs the same suppressions.
@item
The USDT probes are off: FreeBSD's @file{sys/sdt.h} is the kernel's
DTrace SDT header, not the userspace @code{STAP_PROBE} one, and configure
detects that.  @file{tests/dist/test_usdt.sh} reports SKIP.
@item
hegel publishes no FreeBSD @file{libhegel}, so the property tests
report SKIP and @command{make check-sim} exits 77.
@end itemize

@item Linux x86_64 with musl (Ubuntu 24.04, musl 1.2.4, @command{musl-gcc})
@command{make check} and @command{make distcheck} pass.  The
@command{musl-gcc} wrapper has no kernel headers, so configure finds
no @file{linux/futex.h} and a parked worker sleeps on a condition
variable instead of a futex.  The USDT probes are off.

@item macOS arm64 (macOS 26, Xcode clang)
@command{make check} and @command{make distcheck} pass.  Darwin has no
@code{pthread_condattr_setclock}, so a timed wait on a condition is
relative (@code{pthread_cond_timedwait_relative_np}).  macOS has no
@command{timeout}, so the test scripts run without their time limits.
@file{tests/dist/test_symbols.sh} reads ELF symbol tables and reports
SKIP on Mach-O, and the USDT probes are off.  The sanitizer builds and
the property tests are not run on macOS.
@end table
